nvestigator of the Year Award Presentation, Bristol Zoo, 18 January 2019. Association for the Study of Medical Education Researching Medical Education Conference, Friends House, London, 14 November 2018. Results from this paper were presented at the ASME ASM, Aberdeen TECA, 7 July 2022. Author note: The lead author conducted their doctoral thesis on this topic which includes some of work in the submitted paper ( https://doi.org/10.17635/lancaster/thesis/1852 ). The corresponding authors affirm that the manuscript is an honest, accurate and transparent account of the study being reported; that no important aspects of the study have been omitted, and that any discrepancies from the study as planned have been explained. Contributor Information Daniel Darbyshire, Email: d.darbyshire@lancaster.ac.uk. Liz Brewster, Email: e.brewster@lancaster.ac.uk. Rachel Isba, Email: rachel.isba@lancaster.ac.uk. Richard Body, Email: richard.body@manchester.ac.uk. Dawn Goodwin, Email: d.s.goodwin@lancaster.ac.uk. Data availability statement Data are available on reasonable request. References 1. NHS England . NHS long term workforce plan. London, UK: NHS England; 2023. [ Google Scholar ] 2. British Medical Association . London, UK: BMA; 2024. When a doctor leaves: tackling the cost of attrition in the UK’s health services. [ Google Scholar ] 3. Health and Social Care Committee . Workforce: recruitment, training and retention in health and social care. London, UK: House of Commons; 2022. [ Google Scholar ] 4. Warren S. The King’s Fund; 2022. The health and care workforce: planning for a sustainable future. https://www.kingsfund.org.uk/publications/health-and-care-workforce Available. [ Google Scholar ] 5. Shembavnekar N, Kelly E. The Health Foundation; 2023. Retaining NHS nurses: what do trends in staff turnover tell us? https://www.health.org.uk/news-and-comment/charts-and-infographics/retaining-nhs-nurses-what-do-trends-in-staff-turnover-tell-us Available. [ Google Scholar ] 6. World He