INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5463,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12262967','A bibliometric analysis of clinical studies on artificial intelligence in emergency medicine.','Limon Ö, Bayram B, Çetin M, Limon G, Dirican N.','2025','brafford sankey bibliometrix p379 coauthorship haimovich delahanty blomberg mistry raffiz bibliometrix signa bibliometrix bibliometrix bibliometrix bibliometrix bibliometrix coauthorship underexplored kirubarajan onder limon murat gulsum limon onder limon murat nigar dirican onder limon basak bayram gulsum limon nigar dirican onder limon murat gulsum limon nigar dirican onder limon murat nigar dirican basak bayram basak bayram gulsum limon e43282 limon bayram limon dirican e43282 murat muratcetinn nigar dirican nigardirican petrella andresen berlyand dorner caldarini jaf mcgarry jimma telematics gang baltim e42257 bibliometrix articleskeywords scientificandacademicresearch en_us nist dads bradfordslaw cuccurullo bibliometrix informetrics choudhri toerper hamrock haimovich ravindra stoytchev delahanty sherwin blomberg folke ersbøll mistry kelen faridi sprivulis dwivedi raffiz yactayo electr eslava zapata chacon montilla writ chenais lagarde jardine e40031 kirubarajan taher masood boonstra laven combi bellazzi manifesto shamout halsey e286 chamberlin aquino nance pristine neurocomputing amst bartenschlager grieger erber leoss mym neuroergon koza sizing gero sudweeks r108 lamantia platts biese ssw meh perov doorn wptm helmich pmel e43282','Abstract Background: Interest in artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML) has grown rapidly in recent years due to the success of modern algorithms across various domains. Emergency departments are fast-paced and resource-constrained environments where timely decision-making is critical. These characteristics make them ideal settings for the integration of AI technologies, which have shown potential to enhance diagnostic accuracy and optimize patient outcomes. This study aims to identify and characterize the scientific literature on AI and ML applications in emergency departments over the past decade. Methods: A comprehensive search was conducted in the Web of Science database on June 20, 2024. Articles published between 2015 and 2024 were considered. The search was performed using the keywords “artificial intelligence” or “machine learning” in all fields, limited to the “emergency medicine” category. The analysis of the articles included descriptive data on primary publication characteristics, such as the number of authors, citations, country of origin of the coauthors, and journal names. Bibliometric indicators were analyzed to identify publication trends and research themes, cluster analyses of keywords, and thematic maps. Results: A total of 321 articles were analyzed. The average number of citations per article was 10.04, and the annual growth rate was 37.87%. Most publications originated from the United States. Resuscitation, American Journal of Emergency Medicine, Injury-International Journal of the Care of the Injured, and Resuscitation Plus published 107 articles. In 2024, the trending topic of the articles was “health,” while “care” was the most popular in the last 10 years. The top 5 niche themes were “medical,” “digital transformation,” “education,” “database,” and “emergency care systems.” Conclusion: This bibliometric analysis highlights the growing role of AI in emergency medicine. The findings provide insight into current research directions and may help inform future investigations in this evolving field. Keywords: artificial intelligence, bibliometric analysis, emergency medicine, machine learning 1. Introduction In recent years, there has been a marked increase in the interest surrounding artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML), particularly due to their expanding role in healthcare. These technologies are now contributing to critical aspects of medical care, from early disease detection and prognosis to the optimization of therapeutic strategies. [ 1 – 3 ] For clinicians, AI systems offer practical support by predicting treatment outcomes, identifying disease onset, managing clinical complications, and even guiding the selection of effective treatment pathways. One of the most promising benefits of AI is its ability to improve diagnostic precision and accelerate decision-making, sometimes even surpassing conventional approaches in speed and reliability. Emergency departments (EDs) present a unique clinical setting where these technologies can have a profound impact. Characterized by high patient volumes, resource limitations, and the urgency of decision-making, EDs are particularly well-suited for AI-assisted solutions. The ability of AI to quickly analyze and interpret complex clinical data may improve outcomes in these fast-paced environments. [ 3 – 5 ] While the literature on AI in emergency medicine (EM) has expanded, comprehensive evaluations of research trends in this field remain scarce. This is the first bibliometric study of AI in EM publications. In this study, we aim to identify articles involving AI or ML in EM and describe the characteristics of these studies. Through this approach, we mapped current trends, highlighted key thematic areas, and identified opportunities for future exploration in this rapidly evolving domain. In addition, we aimed to identify areas that require greater attention and development. 2. Materials and methods We conducted a bibliometric analysis. The Web of Science (WoS) database was screened on June 20, 2024, to gather relevant articles. The WoS Core Collection was chosen as the sole data source for this study primarily because of its structured metadata and comprehensive citation indexing, which are essential for conducting bibliometric analyses. While other databases also offer valuable content, WoS is widely recognized for its consistency and is frequently used in similar bibliometric studies. Using a single, standardized source helps ensure the reliability of the data and avoids potential issues such as duplicate records or incompatible formats across databases. Given the focus of our study on mapping publication trends and research themes through citation-based metrics, WoS was considered the most appropriate and sufficient database for achieving our objectives. [ 6 , 7 ] The “advanced mode” of WoS was used for the search, focusing on the “emergency medicine” category. The keywords “artificial intelligence” OR “machine learning” were applied as search terms for “All Fields” in the advanced search. The initial 487 publications were refined using filters for the “publication date” and “document type.” Only “articles” published between 2015 and 2024 were considered. Meta-analyses, reviews, letters to the editor, and studies conducted outside the ED were excluded. After applying exclusion criteria, only original research articles relevant to EDs were included in the final analysis. The analysis of the articles included descriptive data on primary publication characteristics, such as the number of authors, citations, country of origin of the coauthors, and journal names. We conducted cluster analyses of Keyword Plus (as defined by Clarivate [ 8 ] as “words or phrases that frequently appear in the titles of an article’s references but do not appear in the title of the article itself”), along with keywords and article titles. These were analyzed based on word recurrence and co-occurrence to form clusters. Inferential analyses, including Brafford law, [ 9 ] were conducted to identify the core cluster of journals with the highest publication volumes. Sankey diagrams were utilized to assess the evolution of research themes over time. Additionally, a word cloud matrix and thematic map were developed to illustrate the bibliometric characteristics of the data. The thematic map categorized themes into motor, niche, emerging or declining, and basic types. Motor themes represented the most extensively studied topics, showing strong connections to other themes. [ 10 ] Niche themes encompassed specialized research areas with strong internal links but weaker external connections. Basic themes represented fundamental yet less-studied topics, while emerging/declining themes highlighted foundational fields or those gaining or losing prominence over the analyzed period. [ 11 ] Statistical analyses were conducted using the Bibliometrix package in R on R-Studio Server (RStudio, PBC, Boston). [ 10 ] This study did not involve human or animal subjects, and thus, no ethical approval was required. 3. Results In total, 321 articles from 43 journals were included in the analysis (Appendix 1, Supplemental Digital Content, https://links.lww.com/MD/P379 ). The total number of authors of the manuscript was 1901, and only 2 manuscripts were published by a single author. The international coauthorship ratio was 23.6%. A total of 8871 references and 844 keywords were used in these articles. The average number of citations per article was 10.04, and the annual growth rate of publications was 37.87%. The 10 most-cited articles are listed in Table 1 . Table 1. Most-cited articles. Authors Article title Times cited, WoS core Times cited, all databases Journal Publication year DOI Taylor et al [ 12 ] Prediction of In-hospital Mortality in Emergency Department Patients With Sepsis: A Local Big Data-Driven, Machine Learning Approach 283 318 Acad Emerg Med 2016 10.1111/acem.12876 Levin et al [ 13 ] Machine Learning-Based Electronic Triage More Accurately Differentiates Patients With Respect to Clinical Outcomes Compared With the Emergency Severity Index 185 200 Ann Emerg Med 2018 10.1016/j.annemergmed.2017.08.005 Haimovich et al [ 14 ] Development and Validation of the Quick COVID-19 Severity Index: A Prognostic Tool for Early Clinical Decompensation 172 175 Ann Emerg Med 2020 10.1016/j.annemergmed.2020.07.022 Delahanty et al [ 15 ] Development and Evaluation of a Machine Learning Model for the Early Identification of Patients at Risk for Sepsis 110 122 Ann Emerg Med 2019 10.1016/j.annemergmed.2018.11.036 Blomberg et al [ 16 ] Machine Learning as a Supportive Tool to Recognize Cardiac Arrest in Emergency Calls 96 102 Resuscitation 2019 10.1016/j.resuscitation.2019.01.015 Mistry et al [ 17 ] Accuracy and Reliability of Emergency Department Triage Using the Emergency Severity Index: An International Multicenter Assessment 83 91 Ann Emerg Med 2018 10.1016/j.annemergmed.2017.09.036 Goto et al [ 18 ] Machine Learning Approaches for Predicting Disposition of Asthma and COPD Exacerbations in the ED 84 87 Am J Emerg Med 2018 10.1016/j.ajem.2018.06.062 Stewart et al [ 19 ] Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning in Emergency Medicine 77 85 Emerg Med Australas 2018 10.1111/1742-6723.13145 Raffiz et al [ 20 ] Optic Nerve Sheath Diameter Measurement: A Means of Detecting Raised ICP in Adult Traumatic and Non-traumatic Neurosurgical Patients 74 83 Am J Emerg Med 2017 10.1016/j.ajem.2016.09.044 Patel et al [ 21 ] A Machine Learning Approach to Predicting Need for Hospitalization for Pediatric Asthma Exacerbation at the Time of Emergency Department Triage 59 63 Acad Emerg Med 2018 10.1111/acem.13655 Open in a new tab Source journal metrics were assessed using Bradford law zones to identify core journals within the category and h-index rankings to reflect their influence on citation counts. Accordingly, Zone 1 of Bradford law included Resuscitation, American Journal of Emergency Medicine, Injury-International Journal of the Care of the Injured , and Resuscitation Plus , which published 107 of the total publications and cited 1079 times in all databases (Fig. 1 and Table 2 ). Figure 1. Open in a new tab Bradford law zone. This figure was created using the Bibliometrix R program. Table 2. Journal metrics according to Bradford law zones and citations. Journal Rank Frequency Zone Times cited in all databases Resuscitation 1 34 Zone 1 481 American Journal of Emergency Medicine 2 33 Zone 1 358 Injury-International Journal of the Care of the Injured 3 22 Zone 1 197 Resuscitation Plus 4 18 Zone 1 43 BMC Emergency Medicine 5 17 Zone 2 127 Journal of the American College of Emergency Physicians Open 6 15 Zone 2 142 European Journal of Trauma and Emergency Surgery 7 14 Zone 2 88 Scandinavian Journal of Trauma Resuscitation & Emergency Medicine 8 14 Zone 2 321 Annals of Emergency Medicine 9 13 Zone 2 705 Emergency Medicine Australasia 10 13 Zone 2 166 Signa Vitae 11 13 Zone 2 24 Academic Emergency Medicine 12 9 Zone 2 442 Pediatric Emergency Care 13 9 Zone 2 20 Open in a new tab The countries with the most significant number of authors appearing in publications were the USA, China, South Korea, Australia, and Germany (Fig. 2 ). Figure 2. Open in a new tab Country ranking for the total number of publications. This figure was created using the Bibliometrix R program. The top 3 Keywords Plus were “care,” “outcomes,” and “survival,” while the author’s keywords were “machine learning,” “artificial intelligence,” and “emergency department” (Figs. 3 and 4 ). Figure 3. Open in a new tab The Keyword Plus’s occurrence. This figure was created using the Bibliometrix R program. Figure 4. Open in a new tab Author’s keyword’s occurrence. This figure was created using the Bibliometrix R program. The trends observed in this study are presented in Figure 5 . In 2024, “health” was the most popular, while “care” was the most popular in the last 10 years. Figure 5. Open in a new tab Trend topics of the articles. This figure was created using the Bibliometrix R program. A thematic map determined using the authors’ keywords is shown in Figure 6 . The analyses revealed that the top 5 niche themes were “medical,” “digital transformation,” “education,” “database,” and “emergency care systems.” The top 5 motor theme clusters were “artificial intelligence,” “clinical decision rules,” “decision tree,” “radiography,” and “data science.” The top 5 basic themes in the articles were “machine learning,” “artificial intelligence,” “emergency department,” “cardiac arrest,” and “deep learning.” “Hemorrhage,” “hospital mortality,” “capnography,” and “predictive model” were the emerging or declining themes (Fig. 6 ). Figure 6. Open in a new tab The thematic map determined by the authors’ keywords. This figure was created using the Bibliometrix R program. 4. Discussion In this study, we analyzed articles published in various journals on the use of AI in EM. From 2015 to June 2024, there was an exponential increase in publications related to AI in EM, from 3 to 321. The exponential growth in publications between 2015 and 2024 reflects AI’s increasing interest and utility of AI in this fast-paced medical field. Similarly, a recently published bibliometric study showed that the number of publications related to AI in medicine is increasing. [ 22 ] This increase in the number of publications highlights the increasing role of AI in medicine, with the potential to significantly transform healthcare delivery. Our study showed that most of these articles were authored by researchers from the United States and China, as previously published different articles revealed. [ 5 , 22 , 23 ] A significant observation of our study is the strong international collaboration, with a coauthorship ratio of 23.6%, indicating a global effort to integrate AI into EM. The most frequently used keywords, in addition to “care,” “outcomes,” and “survival,” included “artificial intelligence,” “clinical decision rules,” and “decision tree.” AI in EM can be applied for various purposes, with current studies predominantly focusing on enhancing decision-making processes. Rapid decision-making and early detection of critical patients are crucial for clinicians in EDs, and the existing literature on AI applications frequently emphasizes these aspects. In this fast-paced medical field, to improve decision-making, diagnostics, and patient outcomes, an analysis of the authors’ keywords highlighted that the top 5 intensively studied themes were AI, clinical decision rules, decision trees, radiography, and data science. Certain “niche” and “emerging” themes identified in the cluster analysis, such as education and predictive models, remain underexplored. This gap may indicate the need for targeted research in less-studied areas. The application of AI to EM emphasizes its potential to transform healthcare by enhancing diagnostic accuracy, imaging interpretation, triage, and decision-making processes in EDs. [ 24 ] Kirubarajan et al [ 25 ] reviewed studies on AI applications in EM and found that most interventions focused on prediction, where AI often outperformed traditional tools and human comparators by effectively processing multiple variables across large datasets. Despite AI’s promise of AI, the current research is largely retrospective and has not yet led to its widespread clinical application. [ 25 ] While many studies have focused on improving patient outcomes and reducing ED overcrowding, mainly using AI in patient triage, [ 26 ] current research seldom addresses specific medical conditions. Consistent with other studies, this study demonstrates that recent investigations into AI applications have predominantly concentrated on triage and clinical decision-making processes. However, AI can also be applied in various areas, such as early diagnosis, error prevention, education, and operational management within EDs. Although the use of AI in medicine has made significant progress toward large-scale implementation, mainly through prospective studies and medical image analysis, it is still in the early stages of validation and application. However, the relatively narrow range of scenarios investigating its impact on clinical environments limits its routine use. [ 27 ] However, numerous factors influence physicians’ decision-making in clinical practice. To ensure that decisions made by AI do not threaten patient safety, AI must provide understandable and explainable outputs that can build trust. [ 28 ] During the COVID-19 pandemic, physicians faced a significant challenge with the surge in ED admissions, where rapid and accurate triage of patients was crucial for effective decision-making. [ 29 ] AI-based diagnostic and prediction models for the progression of patients using chest X-ray and computed tomography images have been developed to assist physicians in the triage of COVID-19 patients. [ 29 – 33 ] Accurate diagnosis and triage are essential for managing high-volume COVID-19 patients, particularly given the limited number of beds available. Integrated human–AI algorithms are recommended for managing critical patients, especially those with reduced intensive care unit capacity. [ 34 ] In situations where the best decision is unclear, humans can rely on AI based on the trust established from previous interactions, suggesting that integrating trust-building strategies into AI algorithms can improve team performance. [ 35 ] Our study found that most articles on AI in the ED focused on patient outcomes. Incorporating AI into triage and diagnostic processes has proven to be effective in enhancing decision-making accuracy and efficiency, particularly in resource-constrained settings, while highlighting the importance of trust-building strategies between AI systems and human clinicians in optimizing overall team performance and patient care. In this study, we identified the concentration of themes related to AI studies in EDs within the authors’ keywords, focusing on AI, ML, and databases. Studies that developed prediction models using ML with large datasets for different patient groups in the ED have reported promising results. [ 12 , 36 – 39 ] However, the number of prospective comparative studies has been limited. [ 40 , 41 ] In the coming years, prospective evaluations of the developed models, focusing on human–computer interaction, could yield critical insights into their applicability and effectiveness in routine clinical practice. 4.1. Limitations As with all bibliometric analyses, this study had certain limitations. First, the WoS database was the sole source used for article retrieval. While the authors considered the WoS to provide a sufficiently large and diverse collection of publications necessary for this analysis, it is acknowledged that citation counts can differ between databases. Although the citation count and annual average citation rate are widely accepted parameters for evaluating a paper’s scientific impact, these metrics alone are insufficient to fully capture a paper’s contribution to the scientific field. 5. Conclusion In this bibliometric analysis, we identified and reviewed articles on the application and use of AI in EM. This study provided an extensive review of the literature on AI in EM over the past 10 years and examined trends in AI research within the WoS dataset. Additionally, the findings offer valuable insights into the current research trends in medical AI, which may help guide future studies in this rapidly evolving field. Author contributions Conceptualization: Önder Limon, Murat Çetin, Gülsüm Limon. Data curation: Önder Limon, Murat Çetin, Nigar Dirican. Methodology: Önder Limon, Başak Bayram, Gülsüm Limon, Nigar Dirican. Writing – original draft: Önder Limon, Murat Çetin, Gülsüm Limon, Nigar Dirican. Writing – review & editing: Önder Limon, Murat Çetin, Nigar Dirican. Investigation: Başak Bayram. Supervision: Başak Bayram. Formal analysis: Gülsüm Limon. Supplementary Material medi-104-e43282-s001.docx (73.3KB, docx) Abbreviations: AI artificial intelligence ED emergency departments EM emergency medicine ML machine learning WoS Web of Science Since this study involved the analysis of previously published data, it did not require ethical approval or informed consent from participants. However, the study was conducted in accordance with the ethical standards set forth in the Declaration of Helsinki. The authors have no funding and conflicts of interest to disclose. All data generated or analyzed during this study are included in this published article [and its supplementary information files]. Supplemental Digital Content is available for this article. How to cite this article: Limon Ö, Bayram B, Çetin M, Limon G, Dirican N. A bibliometric analysis of clinical studies on artificial intelligence in emergency medicine. Medicine 2025;104:28(e43282). Contributor Information Murat Çetin, Email: muratcetinn@gmail.com. Nigar Dirican, Email: nigardirican@yahoo.com. References [1]. Petrella RJ. The AI future of emergency medicine. Ann Emerg Med. 2024;84:139–53. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [2]. Andresen SL. John McCarthy: father of AI. IEEE Intell Syst. 2002;17:84–5. [ Google Scholar ] [3]. Berlyand Y, Raja AS, Dorner SC, et al. How artificial intelligence could transform emergency department operations. Am J Emerg Med. 2018;36:1515–7. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [4]. Caldarini G, Jaf S, McGarry K. A literature survey of recent advances in chatbots. Information. 2022;13:41. [ Google Scholar ] [5]. Jimma BL. Artificial intelligence in healthcare: a bibliometric analysis. Telematics Informatics Rep. 2023;9:100041. [ Google Scholar ] [6]. Liu Y, Gang XK, Gao Y, Wang YX, Wang G. Global status and trends in type 2 diabetes remission from 2002 to 2022: a bibliometric and visual analysis. Medicine (Baltim). 2025;104:e42257. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [7]. Liu B, Zhou CJ, Ma HW, Gong B. Mapping the youth soccer: a bibliometrix analysis using R-tool. Digit Health. 2023;9:20552076231183550. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [8]. Clarivate. ArticlesKeyWords Plus generation, creation, and changes. https://support.clarivate.com/ScientificandAcademicResearch/s/article/KeyWords-Plus-generation-creation-and-changes?language=en_US . Accessed July 1, 2024. [ Google Scholar ] [9]. Black PE. “Bradford’s law,” in Dictionary of Algorithms and