INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(5494,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11180853','Echocardiographic measures of the left heart and cerebrospinal fluid biomarkers of Alzheimer''s disease pathology in cognitively intact adults: The CABLE study.','Hu HY, Hu H, Jiang J, Bi YL, Sun Y, Ou YN, Tan L, Yu JT.','2024','elapse qingdao qingdao δmaximal lvd esv edv ivst pwt lvm 2de qingdao ie33 a4c a2c parasternal plax superoinferior plax diastole lavs δmaximal β42 lvd ivst pwt plax edv esv lvm lvm ivst pwt lvd lvd lvm lvmi lvm a4c a4c a2c qiaamp hilden rs7412 rs429358 polypropylene thaw innotest 181p fujirebio multiskan mk3 gmms mmo mmo bootstrapped gmms δmaximal lvd esv edv ivst pwt lvmi edv esv ivst lvd lvmi pwt mlrs δmaximal δmaximal δmaximal edv esv ivst lvd lvm lvmi pwt 98e 02e 27e 26e 84e 16e 66e 03e 12e 16e 11e 84e 53e 41e 94e 81e 19e 41e 28e 58e 89e 79e δmaximal 45e 52e 44e 33e 57e 07e 59e mm2 85e 90e 40e 70e 01e 11e 11e 87e 55e 10e 25e 04e 05e 41e 44e 41e 15e 29e 32e 98e 15e 77e δmaximal 70e 00e 14e 38e 76e 33e 56e lvd 04e 69e 21e 82e 67e 58e 11e esv 41e 93e 74e 80e 61e 48e 96e edv 96e 48e 68e 24e 05e 82e 30e ivst 81e 47e 78e 06e 48e 96e 70e pwt 87e 75e 95e 59e 29e 87e 24e lvm 36e 03e 06e 46e 25e 09e 71e lvd mm2 98e 51e 06e 16e 09e 42e 65e esv 47e 69e 67e 96e 64e 54e 90e edv 32e 92e 74e 57e 26e 11e 59e ivst 66e 08e 86e 41e 71e 30e 47e pwt 46e 00e 55e 47e 25e 30e 95e lvmi 86e 82e 67e 65e 28e 35e 73e 23e 38e 05e 70e 86e 19e 93e 44e 01e 01e 05e 79e 55e 72e 86e 20e 18e 37e 35e 16e mlrs edv esv ivst lvd lvm lvmi pwt lvd esv edv ivst pwt lvmi lvd esv edv pwt lvmi ivst ivst lvmi mrl ivst lvd lvd ivst ivst lvd ivst lvd ivst lvd lvd edv esv ivst lvd lvmi pwt lvd lvd lvd lvd ivst lvmi gmms edv ivst lvd lvmi pwt δmaximal lvd esv edv ivst pwt lvmi lvmi kresge kresge counterintuitive stiffening ivst pwt lvmi afterload companied monosaccharide glcnacylation glcnacylation lvd edv wmls pathophysiologies lvmi lvmi zimpfer aggravations aggravating qingdao 2022zd0211600 2018shzdzx01 huashan 2022qd002 zhangjiang tianqiao chrissy tanlan jintai_yu twerenbold thomalla volonghi pendlebury scuteri coluccia castello nevola brancati breteler aerqin sevilla bartol bicuspid trileaflet abhayaratna seward norby maheshwari aric e014553 karadag ozyigit ozben kayaoglu altuntas abn6598 feigenbaum inciardi badano avi lyass mitter e285 e290 koscik amst aβ38 neurocogn hurk reijmer hoorn kresge e329 e340 himali beiser gorelick scuteri stomrud forsberg hagerstrom glcnacylation kandiah cdk5 iadecola gottdiener kitzman aurigemma manolio blann hemorheological selvetella notte maffei poppas sakurai mitsui leukoaraiosis manea comsa minca dragos popa hampel hoessler zimpfer wieselthaler czerny asaio immink montfrans koopmans karemaker lieshout','Abstract INTRODUCTION This study delineated the interrelationships between subclinical alterations in the left heart, cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), Alzheimer''s disease (AD) biomarkers, and cognition. METHODS Multiple linear regressions were conducted in 1244 cognitively normal participants (mean age = 65.5; 43% female) who underwent echocardiography (left atrial [LA] and left ventricular [LV] morphologic or functional parameters) and CSF AD biomarkers measurements. Mediating effects of AD pathologies were examined. Differences in cardiac parameters across ATN categories were tested using analysis of variance (ANOVA) and logistic regressions. RESULTS LA or LV enlargement (characterized by increased diameters and volumes) and LV hypertrophy (increased interventricular septal or posterior wall thickness and ventricular mass) were associated with higher CSF phosphorylated (p)‐tau and total (t)‐tau levels, and poorer cognition. Tau pathologies mediated the heart‐cognition relationships. Cardiac parameters were higher in stage 2 and suspected non‐Alzheimer''s pathology groups than controls. DISCUSSION These findings suggested close associations of subclinical cardiac changes with tau pathologies and cognition. Highlights Various subclinical alterations in the left heart related to poorer cognition. Subclinical cardiac changes related to tau pathologies in cognitively normal adults. Tau pathologies mediated the heart‐cognition relationships. Subclinical cardiac changes related to the AD continuum, especially to stage 2. The accumulation of cardiac alterations magnified their damage to the brain. Keywords: Alzheimer''s disease, cerebrospinal fluid, cognition, echocardiography, left heart, tau 1. BACKGROUND In recent years, advancements in heart and brain health research have allowed us to propose a close structural or functional relationship known as the “heart‐brain connection”. 1 Cardiac diseases could accelerate brain aging. Robust data have identified some cardiac diseases (e.g., coronary heart disease [CHD], heart failure, and atrial fibrillation [AF]) and cardiac dysfunction as risk factors of cognitive impairment and Alzheimer''s disease (AD), the most common type of dementia. 2 , 3 , 4 AD is pathologically characterized by the accumulation of extracellular amyloid plaques and intracellular neurofibrillary tangles in the brain, which can be reflected by the cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) biomarkers (amyloid β 42 [Aβ 42 ], phosphorylated tau protein [p‐tau], and total tau protein [t‐tau]) or brain positron emission tomography imaging as early as in a preclinical stage. 5 Despite the underlying pathologies, a prolonged period may elapse before individuals with AD exhibit any clinical symptoms. 6 However, how the left heart measurements influence AD core pathologies has not been fully elucidated, especially in the early stage of AD. Previous research on the associations of cardiac diseases or dysfunction with AD faced some challenges. First, clinically severe cardiac dysfunction would frequently co‐occur with the multiple system dysfunction, which confounded the effects of cardiac dysfunction on AD progression. 7 In addition, although the harms of severe cardiac dysfunction have been reported, fewer cognitively normal (CN) individuals had severe cardiac dysfunction. Therefore, when studying the preclinical stage of AD, focusing on the early and subclinical cardiac alterations is important. Second, previous studies have mostly focused on one individual cardiac disease or parameter, which failed to provide a comprehensive insight into cardiac condition. However, the multi‐alterations in cardiac structure or size were slowly progressive and concomitant, which could reflect a more comprehensive and long‐term hemodynamic or cardiac condition. For example, the increased LV wall thickness could reflect the underlying ventricular hypertrophy, and the increased left atrial (LA) or left ventricular (LV) dimensions could reflect the chamber enlargement, all of which were closely related to the underlying cardiac dysfunction. 8 , 9 Previous studies on electrocardiography (ECG) 10 , 11 as well as cardiac magnetic resonance (CMR) 12 have found the left heart abnormalities to be associated with cognitive decline and dementia. As a commonly used and low‐cost screening tool in the clinical practice, echocardiography can capture both early morphologically and hemodynamically cardiac alterations, which helps to provide a comprehensive structural and functional evaluation of left heart in the subclinical stage. 13 A recent study on echocardiography has reported the association between LA measurements and dementia. 14 However, none of the aforementioned studies restricted the population to have no apparent cardiac dysfunction, nor did they study the multiple left heart measurements or focus on AD pathologies. Therefore, in the present study, we aimed to examine (1) whether subclinical changes of left heart (both LA and LV) on echocardiography were associated with CSF AD biomarkers and cognition; (2) whether the influences of echocardiographic measures on cognition were mediated by AD core pathologies; and (3) whether the accumulation of echocardiographic risk factor had an effect on AD core pathology and cognition in a large sample of 1244 CN northern Han Chinese participants. 2. METHODS 2.1. Study participants All data of participants were obtained from the Chinese Alzheimer''s Biomarker and LifestylE (CABLE) study. Since 2017, the CABLE has been an ongoing large‐scale and independent cohort study aimed at exploring AD''s risk factors and biomarkers in the Han Chinese population. The participants were recruited from Qingdao Municipal Hospital, Shandong, China. The inclusion criteria were (1) self‐reported Han Chinese origin and (2) aged 40–90 years. The exclusion criteria were: (1) major neurological disorders, such as CNS infection, head trauma, epilepsy, and multiple sclerosis; (2) major psychological disorders (such as major depressive disorder and general anxiety disorder); (3) malignant tumors; (4) severe systemic diseases; and (5) genetic disorders. All participants underwent clinical and neuropsychological evaluations, biochemical tests, and bio‐sample (blood and CSF) collection by doctors with standardized training via an electronic medical record system and a comprehensive, structured questionnaire. The CABLE design was approved by the Institutional Review Board of Qingdao Municipal Hospital. In accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki, written informed consent was obtained from all participants or their guardians. In this study, a total of 1244 cognitively intact older adults who were northern Han Chinese were included from the CABLE database. The flow chart of participant selection was detailed in Figure S1 . 2.2. Echocardiographic measures of the left heart Thirteen measurements of LA size, LV size (structure), and LV function were gathered. The LA size included LA diastolic diameter (LAD), minimal LA volume (LAV), maximal LAV, and Δmaximal‐minimal LAV. The LV size and structure included LV diastolic diameter (LVD), end‐systolic volume (ESV), end‐diastolic volume (EDV), interventricular septal thickness (IVST), posterior wall thickness (PWT), and LV mass (LVM). The LV function included systolic function (ejection fraction [EF] and cardiac index) and diastolic function (E/A ratio). The two‐dimensional echocardiography (2DE) was performed at Qingdao Municipal Hospital by trained sonographers using the Philips iE33 ultrasound systems with Vision 2011 software. Images were obtained with participants in the supine position. Standard apical 4‐chamber (A4C) and 2‐chamber (A2C) views were obtained with a focus on the LA and LV zones. Echocardiographic measurements were performed following the recommendations of American Society of Echocardiography and the European Association of Cardiovascular Imaging. 15 All size‐related measurements (diameter and volume) were indexed to body surface area (BSA). Both of the absolute and indexed values were used for the statistical analysis. All participants included in the present study were required to have no obvious cardiac dysfunction. Specifically, they had no obvious LV systolic dysfunction as their EFs were ≥50. 16 Nor did they have severe LV diastolic dysfunction according to the criteria using the E/A ratio combined with the LAV index (LAVI) (e‐Method 1). 17 Participants who were diagnosed as heart failure in the medical history (both electronic medical record system and self‐reported questionnaires) were also excluded. 2.2.1. LA size Linear LA dimensions were measured in three orthogonal planes: parasternal long axis (PLAX), lateral (LAT), and superoinferior (SI) (see e‐Method 2). The anteroposterior LAD was measured in the PLAX view, which is the most widely used linear dimension for determining LA size in clinical practice and research. 15 The maximal (atrial end‐diastole) and minimal (atrial end‐contraction) LAVs were identified on the LA volume curve, which is automatically generated by the software. The average measurements from three cardiac cycles were used. The maximal or minimal LAVI was derived by indexing LAV to BSA. The “Δmaximal‐minimal LAV” was calculated by subtracting the minimal LAV from the maximal LAV to represent the LA reservoir function. RESEARCH IN CONTEXT Systematic review : We reviewed relevant studies in PubMed. Although prior studies have suggested cardiac disease or dysfunction to be associated with cognitive impairment and Alzheimer''s disease (AD), the relationships between subclinical cardiac changes and AD pathologies in cognitively normal adults remains unclear. In addition, previous studies on AD did not use comprehensive measurements of structures and functions of the left heart. Interpretation : Various subclinical alterations in the left heart using echocardiography were associated with not only cognition, but also tau pathologies independently of amyloid β42 (Aβ 42 ). Tau pathologies mediated the heart‐cognition relationships. Accumulation of cardiac alterations magnified their damage to the brain. Our findings indicated that subclinical cardiac changes could affect AD through tau hyperphosphorylation and neurodegeneration. Future directions : Longitudinal studies are warranted to explore the causal relationships between cardiac changes and AD pathologies. Mechanisms underlying the associations between subclinical cardiac changes and AD also need to be studied. 2.2.2. LV size or structure Linear internal measurements of the LV (LVD) and LV wall thickness (IVST and PWT) were performed using the PLAX view (see e‐Method 3). LV EDV and ESV were obtained using the M‐modes. The LVM was calculated from LV linear measurements using the equation LVM = 0.8 × 1.04 ([ IVST + PWT + LVD ] 3 ‐ LVD 3 ) + 0.6. The LVM index (LVMI) was calculated by indexing the LVM with BSA. 2.2.3. LV function The pulsed Doppler (PW) was performed to calculate E/A ratio: the early diastolic filling peak speed wave (E) and the late diastolic peak speed wave (A) were measured in apical projection, with the A4C ultrasound window positioned at the level of the opening apex of the mitral flaps. LVEF was derived using the modified Simpson method in A4C and A2C views. The heart rate (HR) was measured after 5 min of rest using an ECG on the same day as the echocardiography. The cardiac index was calculated as ( HR × stroke volume [SV] )/ BSA . 2.3. Cognitive assessment and APOE genotyping All participants included in this study were required to have no objective cognitive impairment. The global objective cognitive impairment was evaluated by the China Modified Mini‐Mental State Examination (CM‐MMSE; ≤24 for individuals with > 6 years of education, ≤20 for 1–6 years of education, ≤17 for 0 years of education) 18 as well as the Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MOCA; < 24 for individuals with > 12 years of education, < 22 for 7–12 years of education, < 19 for < 7 years of education). 19 The MOCA scores comprise nine cognitive domains: delayed recall, visuospatial ability, naming, attention, executive function, fluency, orientation, calculation, and abstraction. DNA was extracted from blood samples after an overnight fast using the QIAamp DNA Blood Mini Kit (250; QIAGEN, Hilden, Germany). Two specific loci related to apolipoprotein E4 (APOE4) genotypes (rs7412 and rs429358) were analyzed using restriction fragment length polymorphism (RFLP) technology (e‐Method 4). 2.4. CSF AD biomarker measurements and evidence of ATN group CSF was collected by lumbar puncture using 10‐mL polypropylene tubes and sent to the laboratory within 2 h. The CSF was centrifuged at 2000 × g for 10 min. Before testing, the thaw/freezing cycle was controlled not to exceed two times. CSF levels of Aβ 42 , Aβ 40 , p‐tau, and t‐tau were determined with the enzyme‐linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) kit (Innotest β‐AMYLOID [1–42; catalog number: 81583]; PHOSPHO‐TAU [181p; catalog number: 81581], hTAU‐Ag [catalog number: 81579]; Fujirebio, Ghent, Belgium) on the microplate reader (Thermo Scientific Multiskan MK3). All CSF samples were measured in duplicate by experienced operators who are blinded to clinical information. Within‐batch precision values were < 5% for all proteins. The inter‐batch CVs were < 15%. Aβ 42 was expressed as a ratio to Aβ 40 to assess the pathologic species while accounting for individual differences in amyloid production. P‐tau and t‐tau were expressed in ratio to Aβ 42 because they were reported as better predictors of cerebral β‐amyloid deposition than those expressed alone. 20 , 21 The diagnostic criteria for preclinical AD include normal cognition but abnormal AD biomarkers, as proposed by the 2011 National Institute on Aging‐Alzheimer''s Association (NIA‐AA) committee. 22 The three biomarkers in the procedure are aggregated Aβ (Aβ 42 ), aggregated tau (p‐tau), and neurodegeneration (t‐tau). According to previous amyloid imaging and neuropathology studies, 23 , 24 approximately one‐third of cognitively intact older adults exhibit AD pathology. Studies on Asian populations have shown a similar range. 25 , 26 Therefore, the cutoff values for abnormal CSF AD biomarkers were defined as Aβ 42 < 208.57 pg/mL (lower third), p‐tau > 49.80 pg/mL (upper third), and t‐tau > 226.39 pg/mL (upper third). According to the ATN framework, cognitively intact participants were grouped into stage 0 (healthy control [HC], A‐T‐N‐), stage 1 (A+T‐N‐), stage 2 (A+[T/N]+, including A+T+N‐, A+T‐N+, and A+T+N+), and suspected non‐AD pathology (SNAP; A‐T+N‐, A‐T‐N+, and A‐T+N+). Stages 1 and 2 were regarded as the AD continuum (evidence of Aβ plaques). As additional analyses, we also used the AD biomarker cutoffs calculated by Gaussian mixture models (GMMs) and receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curves (Youden index), which have been used in previous CABLE studies. 27 2.5. Covariate measurements The covariates included age (continuous), sex (female or male), educational level (0 years, 1–6 years, or > 6 years), MMSE scores (when analyzing AD core pathologies; continuous), APOE4 status (carriers or non‐carriers), and other dementia or vascular risk factors, including obesity, smoking, alcohol intake, the histories of hypertension, diabetes, CHD, AF, and stroke, as well as the use of anticoagulant and antihypertensive medications (“yes or no” for each). Obesity was defined as a body mass index (BMI) of ≥30 Kg/m 2 . Information on smoking and alcohol consumption was obtained using a self‐reported questionnaire. Hypertension was diagnosed as a systolic blood pressure (SBP) of ≥140 mmHg or a diastolic blood pressure (DBP) of ≥90 mmHg, an antihypertensive medication use, a hospitalization recorded diagnosis, or a self‐reported physician hypertension diagnosis. Diabetes was defined as a plasma fasting glucose level of ≥7 0.0 mmo/L or a non‐fasting glucose of ≥11.1 mmo/L, a hospitalization recorded diagnosis, or a self‐reported physician diabetes diagnosis. CHD was defined by a self‐reported physician diagnosis, prior myocardial infarction diagnosis by ECGs, or ascertained by hospitalization records. AF was ascertained from ECGs, hospitalized medical records, or self‐reported physician diagnoses. Stroke was defined as a history or self‐reported diagnosis of stroke. Data on medication use were obtained from the medical record system and self‐reported medical orders by physicians. Antihypertensive medications included all medications indicated for hypertension. 14 Anticoagulant medications included warfarin, heparin, and oral anticoagulants (rivaroxaban, edoxaban, dabigatran, and apixaban). 2.6. Statistical analysis Characteristics of participants were described using mean ± standardized deviation (SD) for continuous variables and frequency (percentage) for categorical variables. Outlier values, defined as four SDs below or above the group mean, were excluded. The values of the dependent variable in the linear regression models were normalized using the “car” package in R software in cases of skewed distribution. Multiple linear regression (MLR) models were run to associate echocardiographic measures with CSF AD biomarkers and cognition, with echocardiographic measures as the independent variables and CSF biomarkers or cognition as the dependent variables. The MOCA score was used to test global cognitive function and the nine cognitive domains in the analyses. The MLR models were calculated using two models. The basic‐adjusted model (model 1) was adjusted for age, sex, educational level, MMSE scores (when analyzing CSF AD biomarkers), and APOE4 status. The fully adjusted model (model 2) was further adjusted for other dementia or vascular risk factors, as described above. Interaction terms for age, sex, APOE4 , and vascular risk factors were additionally added into MLR models to test their influences on the above associations. The age‐stratified analyses (midlife [< 65 years old; n = 532] and the late‐life [≥65 years old; n = 712]) were performed. Sensitivity analyses were performed after excluding participants with the history of AF or stroke as well as excluding participants who were taking anticoagulant medications. Subsequently, mediation analyses, as proposed by Baron et al., were performed to explore whether the associations between left heart measurements and cognition were mediated by AD pathologies. 28 Mediation effects were established when (1) the left heart measurement was significantly associated with CSF AD biomarkers, (2) the left heart measurement was also associated with cognition, (3) the CSF AD biomarkers were significantly associated with cognition, and (4) the heart‐cognition association was attenuated when the CSF AD biomarker (mediator) was added to the regression model. The indirect effect (IE) was estimated, with the significance determined via 1000 bootstrapped iterations (“mediation” package in R software), and a p‐ value < 0.05 for IE was considered significant. Differences in left heart measurements across the ATN groups were tested using one‐way analyses of variance (ANOVA) and logistic regression models. Logistic regression models were adjusted for age, sex, educational level, and APOE4 status. We also studied the differences in left heart measurements between the HC and AD continuum. To verify the reliability of the results, we repeated the ANOVA and logistic regression models using cutoffs identified by GMMs and ROC curves, respectively. Furthermore, echocardiographic parameters related to AD pathology or cognitive function were identified as the risk factors. For structure or size measurements, only BSA‐indexed values were selected. MLR models were used to test whether the accumulation of risk factors (number of risk factors) in a single individual was related to heavier AD pathology or poorer global cognitive function. The Benjamini–Hochberg false discovery rate (FDR) correction was used for multiple corrections except where specifically noted. A two‐sided FDR‐corrected p < 0.05 was considered statistically significant. R version 4.1.2, 29 SPSS 25.0 and GraphPad Prism 8.0.2 software were used for statistical analyses and figure preparation. 3. RESULTS 3.1. Participant characteristics A total of 1244 individuals who underwent CSF AD biomarkers and echocardiography measurements were included. The participants’ characteristics were summarized in Table 1 . The study participants had a mean age of 65.5 years (SD = 9.2), and 42.8% were female. The population had no obvious cardiac dysfunction with a mean EF of 59.6% (SD = 3.5). The population was cognitively intact (mean MMSE score = 27.5) and included HC (413 individuals), stage 1 (257), stage 2 (109), and SNAP (327) groups. Characteristics of the participants across different ATN stages were shown in Table S1 . TABLE 1. Characteristics of participants. Characteristics No. ( n = 1244) Age (years, mean ± SD) 65.5 ± 9.2 Sex (female, %) 532 (42.8) Education (years, mean ± SD) 9.2 ± 4.2 CM‐MMSE scores (mean ± SD) 27.5 ± 2.3 MOCA scores (mean ± SD) 22.6 ± 4.6 APOE4 status (carriers, %) 173 (13.9) CSF Aβ 42 (pg/mL, mean ± SD) 333.3 ± 220.6 CSF Aβ 40 (pg/mL, mean ± SD) 6750.5 ± 3562.6 CSF p‐tau