s of LA size were positively associated with CSF p‐tau level (FDR‐corrected p = 0.005 for LAD, p = 0.007 for minimal LAV, p = 0.016 for maximal LAV, p = 0.047 for Δmaximal‐minimal LAV; Figure 1B ). Besides, significantly positive associations between all BSA‐indexed LA measures and tau‐related biomarkers were observed (p‐tau: FDR‐corrected p < 0.001 for LAD index, p = 0.002 for minimal LAVI, p = 0.004 for maximal LAVI, p = 0.046 for Δmaximal‐minimal LAVI; t‐tau: FDR‐corrected p < 0.001 for LAD index, p = 0.049 for minimal LAVI; Figure 1A‐B ), except for the borderline correlations of maximal LAVI (FDR‐corrected p = 0.057) and Δmaximal‐minimal LAVI (FDR‐corrected p = 0.061) with t‐tau. FIGURE 1. Open in a new tab Associations of echocardiographic measures with CSF biomarkers of AD pathology. (A) The LA enlargement was associated with high CSF p‐tau and t‐tau levels. (B) The LV enlargement and LV hypertrophy were associated with high CSF p‐tau and t‐tau levels. (C) Associations of all left heart parameters with CSF AD biomarkers were summarized in the heat map. MLR analyses were used after adjusting for age, sex, education, MMSE scores, and APOE4 status. p ‐Values were after FDR adjustment for the multiple corrections. AD, Alzheimer''s disease; APOE4, apolipoprotein E4 ; CSF, cerebrospinal fluid; EDV, end‐diastolic volume; EF, ejection fraction; ESV, end‐systolic volume; FDR, false discovery rate; IVST, interventricular septal thickness; LA, left atrial; LV, left ventricular; LAD, LA diastolic diameter; LAV, LA volume; LAVI, LA volume index; LVD, LV diastolic diameter; LVM, LV mass; LVMI, LV mass index; MLR, multiple linear regression; MMSE, Mini‐Mental State Examination; PWT, posterior wall thickness. We observed the associations of some absolute LA measures with Aβ 40 , which was absent after indexing to BSA. No significant association was found for CSF Aβ 42 , Aβ 42 /Aβ 40 , p‐tau/Aβ 42 , or t‐tau/Aβ 42 (Figure 1B ). After additionally adjusting for the covariates 