ume index; LV, left ventricular; LVD, LV diastolic diameter; LVM, LV mass; LVMI, LV mass index; MLR, multiple linear regression; MMSE, Mini‐Mental State Examination; PWT, posterior wall thickness. 3.2.2. LV measures and CSF AD biomarkers The results of model 1 were shown in Table S2 . Significantly positive associations between all BSA‐indexed LV measures and tau‐related biomarkers were observed (p‐tau: FDR‐corrected p = 0.012 for LVD index, p = 0.013 for ESV index, p = 0.005 for EDV index, p = 0.033 for IVST index, p = 0.018 for PWT index, p = 0.002 for LVMI; t‐tau: FDR‐corrected p = 0.002 for LVD index, p = 0.003 for ESV index, p = 0.003 for EDV index, p = 0.002 for PWT index, p = 0.002 for LVMI; Figure 1A‐B ), except for a borderline correlation of IVST index with t‐tau (FDR‐corrected p = 0.075). As for the amyloid pathologies, some absolute values of LV size were associated with CSF Aβ 40 ; however, the associations were absent after indexing to BSA. The higher IVST index and higher LVMI were associated with a lower CSF Aβ 42 /Aβ 40 (FDR‐corrected p = 0.036, p = 0.045, respectively) or higher t‐tau/Aβ 42 (FDR‐corrected p = 0.026, p = 0.018, respectively) level, respectively (Figure 1B ). No significant association was found for CSF Aβ 42 and p‐tau/Aβ 42 . As for LV function, we did not find any association between LV function parameter and CSF AD biomarker (Figure 1B ). After adjusting for all covariates in the model 2, these results remained stable (Table 2 ). 3.2.3. Influence of Aβ 42 on the heart‐tau relationship To study the influence of Aβ 42 on the associations of left heart measurements with tau pathologies, we repeated the MRL analyses after additionally controlling for CSF Aβ 42 levels, and the associations for tau‐related biomarkers still existed (Table S3 ). In addition, no interaction effect between Aβ 42 and left heart parameter (Aβ 42 × left heart parameter) was found on the tau‐related biomarkers (interaction term p > 0.05). 3.2.4. Interaction effects and stratified analyses Interaction effects between age and left heart parameters (age × left heart parameters) were found on the CSF biomarkers. There was no interaction effect between sex or APOE4 and left heart parameters on the CSF biomarkers (interaction term p > 0.05). As for the vascular risk factors, no interaction effect was found (Table S4 ). Age‐stratified analyses showed that the associations between echocardiographic measures and CSF AD biomarkers were more pronounced in the late‐life (Figures S2–S3 and Tables S5–S6 ). 3.2.5. Sensitivity analyses The associations between left heart measurements and CSF AD biomarkers were consistent after excluding participants with a history of stroke or AF ( n = 99, see Table S7 ) and excluding participants taking anticoagulants ( n = 2, see Table S8 ). 3.3. Left heart measurements and cognition Higher LAD index, IVST index, and LVD index were strongly associated with reduced global cognitive function (MOCA score: FDR‐corrected p = 0.021 for LAD index, p = 0.027 for LVD index, p = 0.015 for IVST index) in model 1 (Figure 2A ). Further analyses of the cognitive domains showed that the echocardiographic measures related to poor cognitive performance were the LAD index, maximal LAVI, IVST index, and LVD index, which primarily reflected the left heart enlargement or remodeling. Specifically, the LAD index was negatively associated with fluency, calculation, abstraction and delayed recall; maximal LAVI was negatively associated with abstraction and attention; the IVST index was negatively associated with fluency and orientation; and the LVD index was negatively associated with fluency and calculation (model 1; p < 0.05 for all; Figure 2B ). The associations of the left heart measurements with MOCA score did not change significantly in the fully adjusted model 2 (Table S9 ). FIGURE 2. Open in a new tab Associations of echocardiographic measures with cognitive function. (A) Higher LAD index, IVST index and LVD index were associated with poorer global cognitive performance. (B) The bubble diagram summarized the associations between the left heart parameters and cognitive domains. (C) CSF p‐tau and t‐tau levels partially mediated the effects of LAD and LVD on cognition. MLR analyses were used after adjusting for age, sex, education, and APOE4 status. The FDR corrections were used for the multiple correction. APOE4, apolipoprotein E4 ; CSF, cerebrospinal fluid; DE, direct effect; EDV, end‐diastolic volume; ESV, end‐systolic volume; FDR, false discovery rate; IE, indirect effect; IVST, interventricular septal thickness; LA, left atrial; LAD, LA diastolic diameter; LAVI, LA volume index; LV, left ventricular; LVD, LV diastolic diameter; LVMI, LV mass index; MLR, multiple linear regression; MOCA, Montreal Cognitive Assessment; PWT, posterior wall thickness. There was no interaction effect between age, sex, or APOE4 and left heart parameters on MOCA score (interaction term p > 0.05). As for the vascular risk factors, no interaction effect was found (Table S10 ). 3.4. Mediation analyses The above findings have showed the enlargement of left heart as a risk factor for cognitive dysfunction as well as a modulator for tau pathologies. Thus, we investigated whether left heart enlargement contributed to global cognitive dysfunction by modulating tau‐related pathologies. In Figure 2C , path A showed that the LAD or LVD index was positively associated with CSF p‐tau or t‐tau levels ( p < 0.05). Path B showed that tau‐related biomarkers were negatively associated with the MOCA score ( p < 0.05). Path C’ showed that the LAD or LVD index was negatively associated with MOCA score ( p < 0.05). The direct path C showed that the heart‐cognition associations were attenuated when tau‐related biomarkers (mediator) were added to the regression model. As the indirect path AB showed, p‐tau mediated part of the heart‐cognition relationship (mediated proportion = 7.5% for LAD index; mediated proportion = 5.4% for LVD index; p for IE < 0.05; Figure 2C ). T‐tau also partly mediated the effect of left heart enlargement on cognition, with mediated proportions of 7.6% for the LAD index and 8.3% for the LVD index ( p for IE < 0.05; Figure 2C ). 3.5. Differences in left heart measurements across AD biomarker profiles Differences in heart measurements among the four AD biomarker categories were noted (one‐way ANOVA, p < 0.05). Heart measurements were significantly higher in the stage 2 and SNAP groups than in the HC group. Additionally, the heart measurements in stage 2 were higher than those in stage 1 (Figure 3 and Table S11 ). In logistic regression analyses, the results did not change significantly after adjusting for age, sex, education, MMSE scores, and APOE4 status (Table S12 ). Moreover, participants in the AD continuum had an increased IVST index and LVMI compared with HC ( p < 0.01). When applying AD biomarker cutoffs identified by GMMs or ROC curves, the main findings from ANOVA and logistic regression analyses did not change significantly (Tables S13–S16 ). FIGURE 3. Open in a new tab Differences in left heart measurements across AD biomarker profiles. In the one‐way ANOVA, differences in heart measurements among the four AD biomarker categories were noted. The LA (A) and LV (B) parameters were increased in stage 2 and SNAP compared to HC. These parameters were higher in stage 2 than in stage 1. p ***<0.001; p **<0.01; p *<0.05; p <0.1. AD, Alzheimer''s disease; ANOVA, analysis of variance; EDV, end‐diastolic volume; HC, healthy control; IVST, interventricular septal thickness; LA, left atrial; LAD, LA diastolic diameter; LAVI, LA volume index; LV, left ventricular; LVD, LV diastolic diameter; LVMI, LV mass index. PWT, posterior wall thickness; SNAP, suspected non‐Alzheimer''s pathology. We explored whether the influences of left heart measurements on CSF tau‐related biomarkers differed by amyloid status (A+ and A‐) in MLR models. The associations between left heart measurements and CSF tau‐related biomarkers were observed in both A+ and A‐ participants (Tables S17–S18 ). Furthermore, among A+ participants, these associations only existed in stage 2 (A+[T/N]+), but not in stage 1 (A+T‐N‐), indicating that the influences of cardiac alterations on tau pathologies were more obvious when tau pathologies have already appeared across the AD continuum (Tables S19–S20 ). 3.6. Trend test for risk factors Ten BSA‐indexed echocardiographic measures that were related to CSF AD biomarkers or cognitive function were identified as risk factors, including LAD index, minimal LAVI, maximal LAVI, Δmaximal‐minimal LAVI, LVD index, ESV index, EDV index, IVST index, PWT index, and LVMI. With the number of risk factor clusters in single individuals, the CSF tau‐related biomarkers (p‐tau and t‐tau) gradually increased, and the MOCA scores gradually decreased ( p for trends < 0.05 for all; Figure 4A‐C ). Additionally, the relationship between the number of risk factors and cognition was partially mediated by tau pathologies ( p for IE < 0.05; Figure 4D‐E ). FIGURE 4. Open in a new tab Trend test for risk factors. With the number of risk factor clusters in single individuals, the levels of CSF p‐tau and t‐tau gradually increased (A‐B), and the MOCA scores gradually decreased (C). The covariates included age, sex, education, MMSE scores (when analyzing CSF biomarkers) and APOE4 status. In the D‐E, path A indicated the associations between the number of risk factors and tau‐related biomarkers. Path B indicated the associations between tau‐related biomarkers and MOCA score. Path C’ indicated the associations between the number of risk factors and MOCA score. The direct path C showed that the associations were attenuated when tau‐related biomarkers (mediator) were added to the regression model. The indirect path AB showed that the relationship between the number of risk factors and cognition was partly mediated by CSF tau pathologies. APOE4, apolipoprotein E4; CSF, cerebrospinal fluid; DE, direct effect; IE, indirect effect; MMSE, Mini‐Mental State Examination; MOCA, Montreal Cognitive Assessment. 4. DISCUSSION This study found in CN adults that various subclinical alterations in the left heart were associated not only with biomarker evidence of tau phosphorylation and neurodegeneration, but also with poorer cognition. Tau pathologies mediated the heart‐cognition relationships. Cardiac parameters were higher in stage 2 and SNAP than HC. The accumulation of cardiac alterations was associated with higher degree of neurodegeneration. This study provided pathological insights into the associations linking AD to subclinical cardiac alterations or subtle function reductions. Our findings were mostly consistent with previous studies. Evidence has accumulated on associations between left heart parameters and cognition. Using ECG or echocardiography, the increases in LAD, 14 LAVI, 11 and LVMI, 4 as well as the dysfunction in LA 10 , 14 and LV, 30 , 31 were correlated with cognitive decline and dementia. Regarding tau pathologies, Kresge et al. 32 found that LV systolic dysfunction, as characterized by reduced EFs, was associated with higher CSF p‐tau and t‐tau levels in CN elders. However, the sample size was small, and the effect of cardiac structural alterations was not studied. Regarding amyloid pathologies, complex findings were reported. Kresge et al. 32 found that reduced EF was related to higher CSF Aβ 42 levels in MCI participants, which seems counterintuitive. It is plausible that in the presence of Aβ accumulation, the compensatory clearance was activated. Studies in CN participants failed to find the associations between hypertension or central arterial stiffening and CSF Aβ 42 . 33 , 34 Compared to previous studies, we focused on the subclinical cardiac alterations, and adopted a comprehensive evaluation of the left heart using larger samples. In this study, higher IVST, PWT, and LVMI were related to heavier tau pathologies and poorer cognition. Under conditions of heavy afterload or coronary artery stenosis, the ventricular walls become thickened to maintain efficient systolic function. However, the LV hypertrophy companied with myocardial ischemia are closely related to the gradually progressive systolic dysfunction, and subsequently, to the reduced cerebral blood flow (CBF). Sufficient CBF is a prerequisite for cognitive functioning, whereas chronic cerebral hypoperfusion (CCH) can initiate neurodegenerative changes and accelerate cognitive decline 35 or AD. 36 Population‐based 37 and animal studies 38 , 39 , 40 have found CCH to be associated with tau hyperphosphorylation and neurodegeneration prior to cognitive deficit. These tau pathologies were mostly found in hippocampus, 38 supporting the role of CCH on AD. Specifically, tau can be normally modified via the attachment of a monosaccharide to prevent phosphorylation, and this modification is called tau O‐GlcNAcylation. However, the tau O‐GlcNAcylation was downregulated in CCH. 38 CCH also exacerbated the blood‐brain barrier (BBB) breakdown, and thus plasma proteins went into brain more easily, causing neuronal toxicity or death. 32 Moreover, the neurovascular coupling, which could ensure the increased local metabolic needs during neural activity and repair the damaged white matter, was impaired in CCH. 41 Although cerebral autoregulation can secure CBF over a range of blood pressures, it would be less effective in the long‐term cardiac alterations, as CCH reduces cerebrovascular reactivity. 42 The dilation of heart chambers, characterized by increased diameters (LAD, LVD) and volumes (EDV, LAVI), was also related to tau pathologies and cognitive dysfunction in this study. The LA enlargement occurs when atrial wall tension is augmented in the volume‐ and pressure‐ overload situations caused by valvular diseases or abnormal ventricular relaxation. 9 LA enlargement reflects the underlying atrial reservoir dysfunction and is a predictor for both systolic and diastolic LV dysfunction. 43 The dilation of LV, caused by chronically volume‐ or pressure‐ overload, is also related to the subsequent systolic dysfunction. 8 Patients might remain asymptomatic despite severely dilated ventricles. First, cardiac chambers dilation was found to promote thrombus formation, and the micro‐embolism led to ischemic brain lesions, underlying the impairment in cognition. 44 Second, the capillary dysfunction and hypoxia could activate inflammatory processes, which promoted the formation of neurofibrillary tau tangles. 45 Third, the dilation of LA 14 , 31 and LV, 46 as well as their following systolic 47 or diastolic 48 dysfunction, were associated with the presence of silent brain infarcts and white matter lesions (WMLs). These cerebral microvascular lesions could amplify the consequences of the neurodegenerative pathology and were risk factors for cognitive impairment and AD. 41 Fourth, cardiac chambers dilation can increase the incidences of AF and stroke, which are established risk factors for cognitive impairment. Nonetheless, after adjusting for AF and stroke in model 2, our results were still significant. Alterations in the aforementioned parameters can reflect the long‐term and underlying cardiac conditions of subclinical dysfunction, hemodynamic alteration, and myocardial injury. 49 Notably, as the alterations in structure or size are chronic and slowly progressive, their harms on the brain would be lasting for a long time. Therefore, by the time cardiac dysfunction or disease become clinically apparent, the damage to the brain might have already occurred. Cardiac alterations and cognitive impairment shared common vascular risk factors and pathophysiologies. Hypertension, dyslipidemia, arterial stiffness, and aging damaged endothelial function and cerebrovascular regulation, providing a pathophysiological background for cognitive impairment and AD. For example, higher LVMI was identified as a reflection for chronic exposure to hypertension, a recognized risk factor for p‐tau deposition 34 and dementia. 4 Besides, the genetic correlations have been found between cardiac structure traits and cognitive function, which supported our results. 12 Moreover, the left heart changes in CMR were associated with imaging markers of brain aging or neurodegeneration, and decreased structural or functional brain connectivity. 12 , 35 CSF t‐tau represents a general damage to axons, while p‐tau represents the hyperphosphorylation and, hence, is more specific to AD than t‐tau. 50 In this study, the associations for p‐tau were more significant, suggesting a possible relationship with AD development. Consistently, the left heart hypertrophy and dilation were observed in the AD continuum, especially in stage 2, which represented late AD pathologies. Cardiac alterations in stage 2 were also more obvious than stage 1, suggesting they had effects on the progression of AD continuum, that meant, accelerated tau pathologies in preclinical AD. The cardiac alterations observed in SNAP indicated they had also harm on non‐AD neurodegenerative diseases. Regarding amyloid pathologies, no association was found between left heart measurements and CSF Aβ 42 . Higher LVMI was associated with lower CSF Aβ 42 /Aβ 40 in all participants, but this association disappeared in age‐stratified analyses. Aβ abnormalities might precede biomarkers of neurodegeneration such as CSF t‐tau, p‐tau, CBF abnormalities, and brain atrophy in the AD development. 51 This temporal difference between changes in CSF biomarker explained why CSF Aβ 42 did not have associations with cardiac changes when p‐tau and t‐tau did. In future, the exact relationships between left heart changes and amyloid pathologies need to be studied. Notably, the heart‐tau relationships did not change after additionally controlling for CSF Aβ 42 in this study. We did not find significant associations for EF, cardiac index and E/A ratio. It was possibly because participants included in this study had EFs of ≥ 50%, which failed to reflect the severity of clinically LV dysfunction. We have also excluded those with severe or decompensated diastolic dysfunction. Therefore, these functional indicators were not enough sensitive to the cardiac alterations in this study population. Zimpfer et al. found that only when systolic dysfunction was severe, the association between cardiac index and cognitive performance was be observed. 52 These findings indicated that the aggravations of AD pathologies and cognitive function originated from subclinical cardiac changes in this study. Age‐stratified analyses showed that the findings were more pronounced in the late‐life. In late life, the increased arterial stiffness was shown to lower the capacity for cerebral autoregulation. 53 The age‐related BBB breakdown was also heavier, especially in the hippocampus, which corresponded to where neurofibrillary tangles first evolved in AD. 32 The strength of this study was the large sample size of CN individuals with information on CSF biomarkers. Besides, we focused not only on the subclinical cardiac changes, but also on the early stage of AD, avoiding confounders in the late stage of these diseases. Moreover, this study had both structural and functional data on the left heart. However, this study has some limitations. First, the cross‐sectional design limited the inference of causality. In future, longitudinal studies are warranted to validate these associations. Second, considering the cost and time of patients, we have not measured LA reservoir strain, conduit strain, and contractile strain. The speckle tracking method should be used in the future. 14 Third, the study did not have data on blood AD biomarker levels. In the future, blood AD biomarkers should be measured to provide a more comprehensive evaluation for the heart‐AD relationship. In conclusion, subclinical alterations in the left heart might contribute to cognitive decline by aggravating tau phosphorylation and neurodegeneration in CN participants, suggesting that we should early monitor the structural or functional cardiac alterations in adults to prevent AD or dementia. The measurements of left heart might help to identify the patients at risk of AD continuum, a target population for preventive interventions for AD. There is a long way to go before integrating echocardiographic parameters into preventive strategies for AD. AUTHOR CONTRIBUTIONS He‐Ying Hu, Hao Hu, Lan Tan, and Jin‐Tai Yu did the manuscript preparation and drafting. He‐Ying Hu, Hao Hu, Jing Jiang, Yan‐Lin Bi, Yan Sun, Ya‐Nan Ou, and Jin‐Tai Yu did the clinical assessments and data acquisition. Lan Tan and Jin‐Tai Yu did the clinical diagnosis. He‐Ying Hu, Hao Hu, and Jing Jiang did the data analysis and interpretation. Lan Tan and Jin‐Tai Yu is responsible for the study conception and design. All authors have contributed to the manuscript revising and editing critically for important intellectual content and given final approval of the version and agreed to be accountable for all aspects of the work presented here. CONFLICT OF INTEREST STATEMENT The authors have declared that no conflict of interest exists. Author disclosures are available in the supporting information . CONSENT STATEMENT The CABLE design was approved by the Institutional Review Board of Qingdao Municipal Hospital. In accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki, written informed consent was obtained from all participants or their guardians. Supporting information Supporting Information ALZ-20-3943-s001.pdf (948.1KB, pdf) Supporting Information ALZ-20-3943-s002.docx (1.4MB, docx) ACKNOWLEDGMENTS The authors thank all participants of the present study as well as all members of staff of the CABLE study for their role in data collection. This study was supported by grants from the Science and Technology Innovation 2030 Major Projects (2022ZD0211600), the National Natural Science Foundation of China (82071201 and 82271475), Shanghai Municipal Science and Technology Major Project (No.2018SHZDZX01), Research Start‐up Fund of Huashan Hospital (2022QD002), Excellence 2025 Talent Cultivation Program at Fudan University (3030277001), and ZHANGJIANG LAB, Tianqiao and Chrissy Chen Institute, and the State Key Laboratory of Neurobiology and Frontiers Center for Brain Science of Ministry of Education, Fudan University. The funding sources had no role in the design and conduct of the study; collection, management, analysis, and interpretation of the data; preparation, review, or approval of the manuscript; and decision to submit the manuscript for publication. Hu H‐Y, Hu H, Jiang J, et al. Echocardiographic measures of the left heart and cerebrospinal fluid biomarkers of Alzheimer''s disease pathology in cognitively intact adults: The CABLE study. Alzheimer''s Dement. 2024;20:3943–3957. 10.1002/alz.13837 He‐Ying Hu and Hao Hu, contributed equally to this work. Contributor Information Lan Tan, Email: dr.tanlan@163.com. Jin‐Tai Yu, Email: jintai_yu@fudan.edu.cn. REFERENCES 1. Janssen E, de Leeuw FE. Getting to the heart of the heart‐brain connection: association or causation? Neurology. 2023;101(2):57‐58. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Jensen M, Zeller T, Twerenbold R, Thomalla G. Circulating cardiac biomarkers, structural brain changes, and dementia: emerging insights and perspectives. Alzheimers Dement. 2023;19(4):1529‐1548. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Volonghi I, Pendlebury ST, Welch SJ, Mehta Z, Rothwell PM. Cognitive outcomes after acute coronary syndrome: a population based comparison with transient ischaemic attack and minor stroke. Heart. 2013;99(20):1509‐1514. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Scuteri A, Coluccia R, Castello L, Nevola E, Brancati AM, Volpe M. Left ventricular mass increase is associated with cognitive decline and dementia in the elderly independently of blood pressure. Eur Heart J. 2009;30(12):1525‐1529. [ DOI ] [ PubMed